@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.19.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  The true-peak reading now tells you how much it cannot see.

  A true peak is the highest point a sound reaches BETWEEN your samples, and it is found by
  reconstructing the waveform and inspecting it on a grid of points. Inseglet's estimator inspects
  every quarter of a sample -- the minimum ITU-R BS.1770-4 allows -- so the nearest point it looks at
  can sit an eighth of a sample away from a real inter-sample maximum, and the number it reports falls
  BELOW the truth by an amount that grows with frequency. It errs in the unsafe direction: it reports
  headroom that is not there.

  WHAT CHANGES.

  Every payload that already reported `truePeakEdgeGuardSamples` now reports two more fields beside
  it. `truePeakOversampling` is the grid factor in use. `truePeakGridBoundDb` is how far a full-band
  sine peak can fall below the truth FROM THE GRID ALONE. The bound is computed from the factor rather
  than written down as a number, so it cannot drift away from the code that produces it.

  THE BOUND SHIPS WITH ITS SCOPE, because a bound whose scope is not stated is a claim rather than a
  measurement. It covers the evaluation grid ONLY. It EXCLUDES the interpolation filter's own
  magnitude error, so the total shortfall on real material can exceed it, and it is derived for a
  sine.

  `truePeakDb` itself is unchanged. The header was compiled before and after the change and read
  IDENTICAL over 12 fixtures, all five EBU Tech 3341 true-peak signals included.

  AND THE METER IS NOW MEASURED AGAINST ALL NINE OF THOSE SIGNALS RATHER THAN FIVE. Cases 20 to 23,
  read from the EBU's own published Loudness test set rather than synthesised in-process, read
  -0.130183, -0.078912, -0.204110 and -0.078911 dBTP against an expected 0.0 -- every one inside the
  +0.2/-0.4 dB window Tech 3341 sets.
